# 🔑 Key Takeaways

* Active Directory and DNS are closely connected.
* Internal AD DNS can work even when external DNS resolution has a problem.
* A Domain Controller should provide the internal DNS service used by domain clients.
* A Windows client should be able to reach the Domain Controller before attempting a domain join.
* DNS should be tested separately from general network connectivity.
* A NAT Network in VirtualBox allows the lab machines to communicate while maintaining Internet connectivity.
* Troubleshooting should isolate the problem layer instead of assuming that Active Directory itself is broken.

---

# 💭 Reflection

Today I moved my home lab from simply having an Active Directory server to creating a small simulated company network.

I connected SERVER01 and WIN11-CLIENT01 to the same VirtualBox NAT Network and configured SERVER01 with a stable IP address. I then tested communication and DNS resolution between the Active Directory environment and the client.

One of the most useful lessons was seeing the difference between internal and external DNS resolution. The lab's Active Directory domain, `kpatel.test`, resolved successfully, while external hostname resolution for `google.com` was not working. This helped reinforce that a DNS server can successfully resolve internal Active Directory records while still having an issue with external DNS forwarding.

The troubleshooting process also reinforced the importance of testing the network step-by-step before attempting an Active Directory domain join.

